  "locus": "Theses in quibus exponitur Doctrina Scholae Romanae circa partitionem Gratiae in sufficientem atque efficacem, & concordiam libertatis humanae cum Gratiae efficacia, CXXV-CXXXIII (the congruist account: efficacy as a congruity and attunement of the divine call with free choice, foreseen by middle knowledge (Petrus a Sancto Joseph, Idea theologiae speculativae 4.7 res. 7; De gratia divina disp. 7 sect. 9 num. 66); God fits the call to temper, time and passion; Tyre and Sidon called incongruously, Ephesus congruously; sufficient and efficacious aid differ only morally, yet the one given efficacious aid receives far greater grace; against Molina, equal grace cannot have unequal outcomes (Bellarmine, De gratia et libero arbitrio 1.13, against Molina and Lessius unnamed; Petrus, aid as being and as benefit, end of disp. 7)).",
